Beryllium: the browser asks the kernel whether each renderer is confined, and ends one that is not

Purpose: plan/18 section 2, stage A (feature matrix S-STA-01; plan/06
  section 3.1, RT-04). Until now only the renderer said whether it had
  locked down: its own log line, and procstat run by the test suite. The
  browser did not ask, so a renderer that skipped its lockdown would have
  been given pages like any other.
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: KernelSaysCapabilityMode(pid) reads
    the process's kinfo_proc (sysctl kern.proc.pid) and returns yes, no or
    unknown from ki_cr_flags & KI_CRF_CAPABILITY_MODE, the field procstat -s
    prints as C. Unknown is every case in which the kernel gives no usable
    answer: the process is gone, or a jail or MAC policy hides it. The
    browser's record of each check (RecordAttestation, WasAttested, and two
    session totals) sits beside the registry of confined peers and is
    forgotten with it.
  - content/browser/renderer_host/render_process_host_impl.cc: when a
    renderer becomes ready (its process is launched and its channel has
    connected, in either order), and the browser launched it with the
    lockdown on (it is a registered confined peer), the browser asks the
    kernel. Anything but yes ends the renderer before it is announced as
    ready: Shutdown(), then SIGKILL, because Shutdown() asks with SIGTERM
    and a process that is not doing what it was asked can ignore that. The
    pid is the one the launcher got from fork, never the one the channel
    reports.
  Why the answer is yes for an honest renderer: RendererMain calls
    EnableSandbox() before it creates RenderThreadImpl, whose constructor
    accepts the Mojo invitation, so the channel cannot connect before the
    lockdown. Every lockdown report since 0.20.3.17 shows one thread at the
    lockdown point, which is the same fact seen from the renderer.
  What it does with --no-sandbox: RendererMain skips EnableSandbox() when
    that switch is present. With the lockdown feature on as well, the
    browser still expects a confined renderer, gets none, and ends each
    one. That is intended (plan/18 section 3.5): the check does not depend
    on what the renderer was told.
  With the lockdown off (the default) nothing changes: no renderer is a
    registered confined peer, and the check returns at once.
Known gap: the window stage B closes. Messages the browser queued before
  the renderer connected are flushed when the launch completes, and an
  unconfined renderer can read them in the moment before it is killed. The
  first navigation can be among them.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch
  (capsicum_rights, the registry of confined peers) and
  beryllium-sender-side-rights.patch (the launcher registers each renderer
  it starts with the lockdown on).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. First
  built in 0.20.3.21 (released 16 November 2026): in the lockdown run on
  the released package the browser attested 7 renderers and ended none,
  and with --no-sandbox it ended all 13.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log
  reads the browser's "attested" and "ended" lines; under the gate, on a
  build that writes them, every renderer that reported its lockdown must
  have been attested and none may have been ended.
  test_an_unconfined_renderer_is_ended starts a browser whose renderers
  are told not to lock down while the browser expects them to, and
  requires that each is ended and that its page never loads.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process confinement)

